// Credentials are never synchronised -- deliberately WITHOUT a leading slash,
// so that the file is excluded at every level
.credentials.json

// Machine-specific patterns, pulled in from a file that does not travel and
// that survives an update. Placed high up on purpose: the first matching line
// decides, so local patterns take precedence over the general ones. Only the
// line for the credentials stays above it. Reasoning in 2.8 of the doc
#include .stignore-local
/.stignore-local

// Whole directories, contents included. /debug, /image-cache and /plans are
// named in the same row of the official documentation, where what you lose by
// deleting them reads "Nothing user-facing". /plans additionally keeps a plan
// carried out on one machine from resurfacing on another as an open task.
/cache
/plugins/cache
/debug
/downloads
/image-cache
/plans
/telemetry
/plugins/marketplaces
/plugins/installed_plugins.set-aside.*.json
/plugins/installed_plugins.unreadable.*.kept
/policy-limits.*

// Register of the marketplaces known locally. Machine-bound twice over: its
// "lastUpdated" records when THIS machine last refreshed the catalogue, and
// every Claude Code start rewrites it, so two machines can never agree on its
// content; its "installLocation" points into /plugins/marketplaces, which is
// excluded above, so the reference travels but the target does not. Four
// rewrites within seven minutes and a threefold conflict on 2026-10-10.
/plugins/known_marketplaces.json

// Download cache of the claude.ai account sync. The server side of your
// account is the source of truth; every signed-in machine fetches these
// trees for itself at start-up and re-checks them every ten minutes. Claude
// Code never uploads from here, and edits made here are overwritten by the
// next round -- so there is nothing to carry between machines. The round
// marker ".last-complete-round" inside each tree holds a locally generated
// round id plus the client name, so two machines can never agree on its
// content: it conflicted three times within four seconds on 2026-10-01.
/plugins/synced
/skills/synced

// Where the same sync parks what it removed. Machine-bound, swept by
// cleanupPeriodDays, and recoverable only on the machine that lost the file.
/plugins/.trash
/skills/.trash

// Machine-bound: backup copies, session markers, environment, shell captures,
// IDE connection
/backups
/ide
/sessions
/session-env
/shell-snapshots

// Sessions whose working directory was the scratchpad under /tmp. The path
// carries the session id, so it never becomes a working directory again, and
// after a reboot it no longer exists locally either. A directory pattern takes
// its contents with it; the second line is belt and braces, because this one
// carries a wildcard.
/projects/-tmp*
/projects/-tmp*/**

// Snapshots taken before every edit, for /rewind. Machine-bound and
// dispensable; the function is replaced by the checkpoint commits.
// Reasoning and the price of this exclusion in 1.3 of the doc
/file-history

// Timestamp of the last local housekeeping sweep, governed by
// cleanupPeriodDays. The value travels, its meaning does not: it asserts that
// THIS filesystem was swept, which is false anywhere else, and an incoming
// newer stamp makes the receiving machine skip a sweep it never ran. Rewritten
// at every Claude Code start with millisecond resolution, so two machines can
// never agree on its content: two conflict copies within one second on
// 2026-10-10, 2.7 seconds apart from the original.
/.last-cleanup

// Outcome of the last self-update of THIS installation: install path,
// success or failure, and the versions before and after (e.g. "native",
// 2.1.304 -> 2.1.308 on 2026-10-23). The same trap as /.last-cleanup: the
// statement is true only for the machine that ran the update and false
// anywhere else. Not listed in the official description of ~/.claude, so the
// exclusion rests on the content, not on documentation.
/.last-update-result.json

// Prompt history and the pasted text it refers to. Documented as "Prompts
// you've typed, with timestamp and project path. Used for up-arrow recall,
// Ctrl+R history search, and ! shell-command completion"; paste-cache holds
// the "Contents of large pastes", and deleting it costs the "Pasted text in
// recalled prompts". Resume, continue and rewind do not
// depend on either. Every prompt on every machine appends a line to the same
// file, so parallel use on two machines ends in a conflict copy; one was
// examined on 2026-10-23. The price of excluding: each machine recalls only
// the prompts typed on it. paste-cache goes along, because without the history
// that refers to it, its files are useless elsewhere.
/history.jsonl
/paste-cache

// Local cache of the organisation's managed settings, written by Claude Code,
// with its signature and fetch time. ".synced-at" holds the millisecond time
// of a fetch, so two machines rewriting it can never agree on its content:
// two conflict copies per file within one second on 2026-10-22.
/remote-settings.json*

// Transient files
*.log
*.sock

// Synology recycle bin, if enabled in the target folder
#recycle
